  {
   "id": 18,
   "type": "MarkdownNote",
   "pos": [
    -1240,
    -324
   ],
   "size": [
    720,
    306
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 0,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## pose-skeleton.json\n\nPose from a skeleton\n\n**Does:** puts the character in a POSE you supply as a skeleton - ControlNet-Union's `openpose`\nmode drives the pose while IPAdapter carries the design.\n**Skeleton in:** the ControlNet reads node 1. Author one with\n`scripts/pose_probe.py draw <keypoints.json>` (edit an extracted keypoints file, or write the\njoints by hand) and upload it to `input/_pose/`. `OpenposePreprocessor` is wired to a reference\nimage but left **DISCONNECTED**: connect its IMAGE output to `ControlNetApplyAdvanced.image` to\ntake a pose from a photo instead - it finds no person in stylised art, so photos only (E21).\n**Knobs:** CN `strength` 0.9 (the pose follows; the DESIGN drifts). IPAdapter `weight` **0.35**\nholds the design - do NOT raise it expecting a closer character: at >=0.6 the reference's own\npose wins and your skeleton is ignored (E21/E11).\n**Limits:** a 2D COCO skeleton has no yaw, so a **camera angle** change is not this flow's job -\nuse an instruction edit, or the 3D path (`Hy3DCameraConfig` + `Hy3DRenderMultiview`).\n\n---\n*How to run it: story **S7** in `HOWTO.md`  ·  Why it is this way: **E21** in the scenarios' `EXPERIMENTS` logs.*"
   ],
   "color": "#332922",
   "bgcolor": "#593930"
  }
